            "review_title": "Review at Che Thai Y Phuong",
            "review_text": "The_Pale_Ridah\nGladesville, Australia1925 contributions\n0\nThe local’s hidden secret\nSep 2014 • Couples\nBeing a local, this place is the most popular place on Nguyen Chi Phuong road - a Chinese neighbourhood, making Che Thai Y Phuong basically the king around here. A popular night desert hangout for young people they specialise in desert beverages & in particular a cold coconut milk-type sweet desert called ‘Chè Thai’ - it has soft jelly goodies, exotic Asian fruit pieces & is chilled with shaved ice. Without doing the description any justice all I can add is that this place does pretty good iced deserts that are cheap. It is so popular they have taken up 3 shop spaces & extend all the way out into the footpath that is usually crowded until 10pm every night.\n\nSam Bo Luong – aka Ching Bo Leung in Hong Kong is an exotic sweet beverage that would impress your Asian girlfriend to infinity. Only cool foreigners would even try this but every Vietnamese expat kid growing up in the west knows it well. Sam Bo Luong is a sweet drink containing exotic Asian weird things such as red jujube dates, longans, lotus seeds, job’s tears & thinly sliced sea weed with crushed iced over the top half of a long glass. If it doesn’t sound appealing, it just means you don’t have an Asian girlfriend.\n\nOne thing about Che Thai Y Phuong is that since it is outside of the touristy District 1 virtually no foreigners ever get to visit local desert places like this. For a tourist, it would be ambitious to venture outside of that area to sample part of Saigon’s local food culture. Unlike cafes or go-go bars, there are no female wenches here so it doesn’t attract shady Vietnamese men or males over the age of 30. It’s just desert – the good old fashion clean type for a younger crowd. That’s why I like it here, no hawkers, touts or tourists. The big old lady-owner wobbles around carrying a large stack of cash is always cranky, she barks at her staff all night, very entertaining. The deserts are relatively cheap at around 18,000 VND up to 33,000 VND or less than $1.50 USD & the atmosphere here is always lively. \n \nFrom District 1, a taxi fare to Nguyen Chi Phuong road in District 10 is approx. 60,000 VND or $3.00 USD. Locally known simply as Che Thai (chair thai), there is another competing Che Thai a few shops down that uses bright Green fluro lighting & chairs, although it is ok it is nowhere near as popular as Che Thai Y Phuong.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nAtmosphere\nWritten November 20, 2014",
